From 3e74ccaba6157705f34d9d6515aee05bbbba503d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 20:42:34 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 32/88] box: let the Go memory limit follow the memory the
 system can still give

Without a limit the collector lets the heap grow to twice its live size before
it runs, so under load a router with little memory met the OOM killer. The soft
limit is now the memory of Go plus what the system can still give (MemAvailable,
or the room under the limits of the cgroup and its parents with the file cache
they can reclaim, tmpfs and shared memory left out, whichever is less) minus the
most of our own code ever resident: the memory of Go at which the kernel would
have to evict our code. Our own allocations do not move it; when others leave
the system less than our code it drops below the memory Go holds by the
shortfall, never below what the collector and the scavenger cannot give back
plus the smallest heap goal of the runtime (4 MiB at GOGC=100), with less room
the collector would run again on the first allocation after every cycle. It is
computed again after every garbage collection, from files read into one buffer.
GOMEMLIMIT or experimental.debug.memory_limit set by hand switch it off.

In a 128 MiB container under a download load with 72 MiB taken by another
process, the kernel killed a process (sing-box itself in one of two runs)
without the limit and none with it, at the cost of a slower transfer while the
memory was short; with memory to spare CPU and RSS are unchanged.
